    "result_text": "All 9 test classes ran (17 tests) and the build is green. \n\nThe change is complete. Here's what I implemented to satisfy the `customerCode` specification:\n\n## Summary\n\n**Spec:** Assign `customerCode` formatted `<LAST3>-<NNNN>` \u2014 `LAST3` = upper-cased first three letters of `lastName`, `NNNN` = a global 4-digit zero-padded sequence equal to one more than the current number of owners (e.g. `SMI-0007`).\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **New pipeline step** \u2014 `rest/function/owner/AssignCustomerCode.java`: reads the built `Owner` via `@Val`, computes the code as `String.format(\"%s-%04d\", prefix, ownerRepository.findAll().size() + 1)`, and mutates the owner in place. It runs before the owner is saved, so consecutive creates get consecutive numbers.\n\n2. **Wiring** \u2014 `officefloor/rest/api/owners.POST.yml`: inserted `assignCustomerCode` between `build` and `save`, under the same `transaction` governance.\n\n3. **Entity** \u2014 `model/Owner.java`: added a `customer_code` column field with getter/setter.\n\n4. **Schema** \u2014 added `customer_code` to the `owners` table in all four DB schemas (hsqldb, h2, mysql, postgres); hsqldb is what the tests use.\n\n5. **API contract** \u2014 `openapi.yml`: added a `readOnly` `customerCode` property to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` carries it and MapStruct maps it by name onto the `GET /api/owners/{id}` response.\n\nDesign notes: the code must be persisted (not derived at read time) because the sequence depends on creation order \u2014 deriving it on GET would give both owners the same value. Placing `AssignCustomerCode` after `BuildOwner` and before `SaveOwner`, and counting owners before the save, makes each new owner's number exactly one greater than the previous \u2014 which is what `Cp09Tests.coreFormatAndGlobalSequence` asserts (`SMI-...` / `JON-...` with `cb == ca + 1`).",
